    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking at some testing data for a new high-performance braking setup. Can you compare the thermal dissipation rates and heat soak characteristics of ceramic-matrix composite (CMC) pads versus standard semi-metallic pads during a high-speed deceleration event? I'm specifically interested in how the thermal gradient affects the rotor surface during repeated heavy braking cycles.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ceramic-matrix composite (CMC) pads generally exhibit superior thermal dissipation due to their lower thermal conductivity and higher operating temperatures, resisting rapid thermal buildup. Standard semi-metallic pads tend to generate higher localized heat, leading to quicker and more pronounced heat soak into the rotor during repeated cycles. This means CMC pads often maintain a more stable and lower thermal gradient across the rotor surface during heavy, repeated braking. Therefore, CMC offers better consistent performance under extreme thermal stress.",
      "source": "target",
